    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "86c9448a-f157-3bec-b1a9-223348d80b3d",
                "word": "Gloriana",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ef1d2f0b-1891-3a97-9be0-7da627049598",
                        "entry_id": "86c9448a-f157-3bec-b1a9-223348d80b3d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Sobriquet for Elizabeth I (1533–1603), Queen of England and Ireland."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9ee5ec71-e443-45f6-8087-e80b5e912958",
                                "sense_id": "ef1d2f0b-1891-3a97-9be0-7da627049598",
                                "text": "In “The Sayings of Queen Elizabeth” (The Bodley Head; 16s.), Mr. Chamberlin deals very faithfully with a scandal that has nothing to do with sixteenth-century gossip about the Queen’s morals. It is a purely academic outrage: Froude’s Bowdlerisations of Gloriana’s good things, which the historian paraphrased loosely when he should have transcribed them accurately."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f4d65367-243c-4df2-aa7b-cbcfd27ee843",
                                "sense_id": "ef1d2f0b-1891-3a97-9be0-7da627049598",
                                "text": "The cult of Gloriana was skilfully created to buttress public order and, even more, deliberately to replace the pre-Reformation externals of religion, the cult of the Virgin and saints with their attendant images, processions, ceremonies and secular rejoicing."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aae0a2b0-41e8-44fc-84ed-a903873fbf95",
                                "sense_id": "ef1d2f0b-1891-3a97-9be0-7da627049598",
                                "text": "In Spanish eyes, the Great Elizabeth was neither ‘Gloriana’ nor ‘Good Queen Bess’, but ‘la inglesona’."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "29eff80b-d10b-490a-bd33-2fdf2fdb09cb",
                                "sense_id": "ef1d2f0b-1891-3a97-9be0-7da627049598",
                                "text": "In Hymnes to Astraea (1599) John Davies characterised Gloriana’s rule as captivating her subjects with an authority based on honour and ‘straight rule’: […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ce628f20-9583-4da5-bda6-8090b0e163b1",
                                "sense_id": "ef1d2f0b-1891-3a97-9be0-7da627049598",
                                "text": "After the early seventeenth century, such cruelty would never be seen again in England, but it was part and parcel of the reign of Gloriana and the gentlemen of the Privy Council kept it that way."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e045145f-15ab-410c-8fb1-0993f0c4cecd",
                                "sense_id": "ef1d2f0b-1891-3a97-9be0-7da627049598",
                                "text": "At first she was the aloof, mysterious beauty of the courtly love tradition, later the magnificent unattainable Virgin, Gloriana."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "414cabfc-4952-37cd-9160-8a850ff2a7bd",
                        "entry_id": "86c9448a-f157-3bec-b1a9-223348d80b3d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A female given name."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9f809da3-8b44-4e58-bebc-ee6c1d477c0f",
                                "sense_id": "414cabfc-4952-37cd-9160-8a850ff2a7bd",
                                "text": "After Mr. Smith’s decease, she m. for a 2d husband, and as his 2d wife, Dr. George Muirson of Setauket, L. I. (son of Rev. George Muirson of Hempstead, L. I., and Gloriana Smith, dau. of Col. William Smith)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c1f42224-d358-4619-a924-4bbc53d24c2c",
                                "sense_id": "414cabfc-4952-37cd-9160-8a850ff2a7bd",
                                "text": "The little stranger is named Gloriana Craig. She arrived on June 24, 1922."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5f54abc9-dcd5-4b11-bfcd-d0530969619d",
                                "sense_id": "414cabfc-4952-37cd-9160-8a850ff2a7bd",
                                "text": "When I was the ripe age of 17, I met a girl named Gloriana at an out-of-state sports meet."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "521c4828-6a3d-4460-81af-aadbb70d6fd1",
                                "sense_id": "414cabfc-4952-37cd-9160-8a850ff2a7bd",
                                "text": "Through an acquaintance with his mother’s neighbor, Robert Bourassa, he met a woman named Gloriana LaPointe."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
